  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single platform in a single Chinese metropolis, which may limit generalizability to other cultural contexts or platform structures.",
      "what": "Using a dataset of 373,964 bedroom listings over one year in Nanjing, China, this study analyzed how roommate gender composition affects rental prices on Ziroom, the nation's largest shared housing platform. Hedonic regression models controlling for unit, building, and neighborhood features revealed a rent premium for all-female shared units, which is driven not by platform-set base prices but by gendered demand patterns interacting with algorithmic pricing during active leasing and subleasing phases.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that algorithmic pricing systems on housing platforms can monetize social preferences like gender homophily, translating a desire for same-gender living arrangements into a financial penalty that disproportionately affects female renters."
    }
